[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Elasticsearch Cluster Architecture, Nodes, and Shards"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Elasticsearch is a highly scalable, distributed search and analytics engine. To effectively design, scale, and maintain an Elasticsearch deployment, it is crucial to understand its core architectural components: Clusters, Nodes, and Shards. These components work together to provide high availability, fault tolerance, and rapid search performance across massive datasets."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. The Elasticsearch Cluster"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A cluster is a collection of one or more node instances that share the same cluster name. Together, they hold your entire dataset and provide federated indexing and search capabilities across all nodes. The cluster's state is managed dynamically, ensuring that if nodes join or leave, the workload is automatically redistributed to maintain balance and availability."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Node Roles and Responsibilities"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"A node is a single running instance of Elasticsearch. While every node can perform all tasks by default, in production environments, nodes are typically assigned specialized roles to optimize resource utilization:"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Master-eligible Node: Responsible for cluster-wide actions, such as creating or deleting an index, tracking which nodes are part of the cluster, and deciding which shards to allocate to which nodes."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Data Node: Holds the shards that contain the documents you have indexed. These nodes handle data-related operations like CRUD, search, and aggregations, requiring high CPU, memory, and I/O."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Ingest Node: Enables you to run ingest pipelines to transform, enrich, and preprocess documents before they are indexed."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Coordinating Node: Acts as a load balancer that routes incoming client requests, distributes search queries to data nodes, and merges the results into a single response."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Shards and Replicas"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"An index is a logical namespace pointing to one or more physical shards. Elasticsearch subdivides an index into multiple pieces called shards. Each shard is a self-contained, fully-functional instance of an Apache Lucene index. Primary shards handle write operations and act as the original home for your data, while replica shards provide redundant copies to prevent data loss and serve read requests."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"PUT /my-application-logs
{
  "settings": {
    "number_of_shards": 3,
    "number_of_replicas": 2
  }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Shard Routing and Allocation"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"When a document is indexed, Elasticsearch determines its destination shard using a simple routing formula: shard = hash(_routing) % number_of_primary_shards. Because of this math, the number of primary shards in an index cannot be changed easily after creation without reindexing. Replica counts, however, can be modified dynamically at any time."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"GET /_cluster/health?pretty"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Monitoring cluster health is vital. A 'green' status indicates all primary and replica shards are allocated. 'Yellow' means all primary shards are active, but some replicas are unallocated (often seen in single-node setups). 'Red' indicates that one or more primary shards are missing, meaning some data is temporarily or permanently unavailable."}]
